WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
What? So — welcome back. Good. Okay.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.000
So, we left off having filed an application (beantragt).

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
'Applied-for' means we sent it to the foreign tax authority, which now has all the documents and must decide whether to transfer the money.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
And some months later, a year later, the money comes. It arrives at some point and is imported into RaQuest as a payment receipt (Zahlungseingang).

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The data comes in, and this payment receipt — which we met earlier under 'Zahlungseingänge verwalten' — would be matched fully automatically to the application.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.000
But what we could do is look up this application separately and create a manual payment receipt. Let's do that. I click close.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
I go to RaQuest's main page, to Anträge → Anträge verwalten. Because we know an application was already created from the claim. So I go to Anträge verwalten.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
And now I want the application I just created. How do I get it? I search by a filter: date, status change from.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
So a status was changed today.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.000
And here we have this application with its number, on status 'applied-for'. 10,000 Swiss francs.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
And the status timestamp is 5/5/2021 — today, right? Great. Now a payment receipt comes in, a few months later.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:07.000
I'll simulate it manually now, since I don't have the data handy. So I right-click → 'create payment receipt for application'. A new mask opens that signals to me:

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
maybe something's still missing — sender account. Not sure I really need the sender account. Let's see if it works anyway.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
So it generated a payment receipt for me with a reference number, again with the amounts. And I may have to convert Swiss francs. So now I need a booking entry.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
You can check online: what's one euro in Swiss francs? One second. One second.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Bankers can of course pull their exchange rates from within the bank themselves.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Euro, Swiss franc. One euro is 1.1 Swiss francs. So I enter 1.1 here, as the current exchange rate — can you hear me?

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:33.000
[audio issue] In the room I can't hear myself speak. That's on purpose. I can't hear you.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Anna, can you check your device settings again? We can't hear you.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
[audio troubleshooting] We share an external speaker in the room.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
And it runs in parallel with the TV speakers. That's why... Anna, yes, there. Now everything runs through the device. You're showing us now?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Did you catch it all? Yes, I could always hear you. Okay, so now we see.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.000
[audio troubleshooting]

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.000
You just have to cross out the mic on your Teams. And I'll cross mine out too. Now it should work, right?

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Yes, I don't hear myself anymore. Okay. So this payment receipt now has to be converted — Swiss francs into euros.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.000
The case workers know their exchange rates. I looked it up online. And they enter that rate here. And you see, ah, there's an original amount, 10,260.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
Converted to euros that's probably 9,328. And then I click Eintragen (commit). Hopefully it works.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
A bit to the right here. Scroll onto this green blob. And Eintragen again. Yes, accepted.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Now let's watch this status, the second-to-last column, 'applied-for'. If you wait a bit and the calculations run through, then on refresh

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
— I always do it with Suche starten — it should go to 'erstattet' (refunded). Refunded is the next logical status. Let's see. Nothing came.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
[system busy] I went to the context menu. Maybe I should resolve this error. But I think the sender account may not be that important.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
But if I know the sender account is the Swiss tax office, I could enter it anyway. Doesn't hurt.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
I look up the tax office in Switzerland.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Let me go to root-cause investigation (Ursachenforschung).

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
[creating account] Apply. Let's try once more. And otherwise —

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
— it's not dramatic. The test instance I'm using for the first time. Stay calm.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
'Create payment receipt for application'. We're back at the application. And we have to enter a booking entry. Here it comes.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.000
I go right onto the green blob. And then Eintragen. Well.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
It doesn't like me.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Never mind. What would happen now would be —

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.000
— that these statuses aren't in here anymore, that it creates a new status called 'erstattet' (refunded). And that means: the money came from the foreign tax authority,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:14.000
onto the bank's account. And later, when you pay out to the customer, you get a new status called 'vergütet' (paid-out). Remember it as: refunded = to the bank.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Paid-out = to the customer. A very important status for the future. That's basically the whole process. We took a claim,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
wrote to a tax office in France, let the process advance to the filing office, filed in Switzerland with the foreign tax authority.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
And then we waited for the money. The money came, would ideally now be matched to the application, would have triggered a new status,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.000
which would then be called 'refunded'. That'd be the whole process. And then you'd pay out to the customer — that would be 'paid-out'.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.000
In the training I won't go further into root-cause analysis now. Otherwise I'd resolve this problem here — it's surely simple and trivial. But so you can take away as much as possible today,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.000
we deliberately won't do the analysis now. So in the end you could say, hey, my target status is 'paid-out'.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And then you have lots of applications on that 'paid-out' status. And then it's done — nothing more to do with them. But all these intermediate statuses you have to go through.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:00.000
You have to let the process advance. — Can I actually make a report for each status? If I'm a case worker, I want to know: okay, what's applied-for?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
What's in the reporting? And so on. — And the ones that are finished, can I push them all into an archive? — [No.]

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Example: the customer could have more addresses. Say his registered address is here, but he has a domicile — a holiday home — in Austria,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
then he'd have a second address, and in Austria he could have his mail sent, because he checks his securities on vacation.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Then he'd have a second address, and on the second one there'd be a green check for 'default mailing address'. If you want to edit it, double-click, edit.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.000
As a rule, case workers don't have to edit anything here, because the data comes from the core banking system, was imported into RaQuest.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.000
So it's always triggered by an upstream system that's much smarter and knows much more.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.000
When an address change comes — he moves from Bad Wörishofen to Rosenheim — a new address flies into the system, a new record, and here you'd get an old record and a new record, an update with the new address.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.000
And ideally he'd also have a new registered address where he's now taxed in Rosenheim. This should all happen automatically — not the case worker maintaining 10,000 addresses.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.000
God forbid, no. But I do it entirely myself when I run many tests or a business analysis — I assemble the data myself. So there I'm completely free.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.000
And you find all this — address etc. — under 'General'. Now there are more such sections.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:28.000
If the customer were somewhat wealthy, he might have a tax advisor or someone as his contact person, which you could store.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.000
There are certain address roles you could store — I don't need to go into that in detail.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.000
But very important are the bank connections, because he may have a depot clearing account — an account where his money eventually appears if he has it calculated out.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
And he may have a depot — you see that under Depot overview, you can look in again. And he may also have tax data — not just may, he has it.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.000
And his tax data is valid from 1/1/2005.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.000
You could infer he's lived in Bad Wörishofen since 2005 and is registered for tax there with a tax number, this figure.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.000
And, not entirely traceable, but his tax office would be Rosenheim with branch office Wasserburg. These are test data. So you already have quite a lot here.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
You have the tax number, the email, you know the tax office — all known on the contact. There are also validity histories (Gültigkeitsverläufe). I could say, well, he moves three times.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Then a new validity period would go in each time. For that I'd make a new entry. From 2005 he was actually in Wörishofen. But on 5/5/2021 he's elsewhere.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.000
And let's do that briefly now, because afterwards I want to show you something important for the training. Add new entry.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.000
And I say, a registered address is no longer in here. I have to add a new one. Back to Basic. I go back to General.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Now I say it flutters into the system. Let's simulate it. Germany. Rosenheim. Postal code.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.000
That should do for now. Street too. Now a new address flutters in here. The first is the registered address.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.000
It has a green check. The second not yet. What do we do? Let's go to tax data. And see if we can turn something there. Add new entry. Ah, Meierstraße is selectable.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Great. Selected. Now I have to pick a new tax office. Here the tax office is queried. I press Ctrl+S.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.000
That's just trying, because I'm searching for a new tax office. Let's take one in Neukirchen.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And from when? From 5/5/2021. Then I click Eintragen. And now we already see a validity. We see: until 5/5 he was registered for tax in Bad Wörishofen. Or actually 4/5. And from 5/5.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Including 5/5 — I'd say it's valid. One should say: including 5/5 he's resident and registered for tax at this new address.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Now I click Übernehmen (apply). And now something happens in RaQuest. Let me go to the main page. I hope it happens too.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Or are the services down there? What a shame? Ah, something's coming. A four-eyes principle (Vier-Augen-Prinzip) suddenly pops up — it says 'state under review'.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:18.000
Today at 11:59 for Oskar Daniel, the customer we edited. What's the four-eyes principle in banking? A case worker can't just change data without a supervisor seeing it.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Or a colleague. So it means someone else has to accept it. I now have so much power and so many roles that I can accept myself.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But if I were the case worker, then the second person — who also has a RaQuest application — would have to accept it now. So I accept.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.000
Only with acceptance is this new address valid. So I go back into the contact, click Suche starten, and pull up Oskar Daniel.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.000
So, now he has two registered addresses. How can that be? Well, of course.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.000
He was resident in a different period than there. If he had claims from 2005, 2006, 2007, he'd have been registered for tax and resident there.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.000
And that'd be the tax validity for the earlier years. From 5/5, if he got shares — dividends — after 5/5, the Rosenheim address would be valid.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.000
So there are two valid, parallel-existing addresses, separated only by a validity. Why did I explain this validity thing now?

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
In RaQuest we have many facets with these validities. So you may — should — memorize this.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.000
Hey, behind very many things there may also be a time validity. Because things simply change often. And the database remembers that.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.000
So it knows: at that time it was like this, at that time like that. There's a status history — or state history you could say.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:05.000
That's the tax data on the customer. Extremely important. Address extremely important. Depot overview — extremely important. Because every contact must have a depot to trade at all.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.000
This customer happens to have two depots. The depot numbers are a bit truncated. They have two different numbers, because there's a '1' in there. Good.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Now I can say, okay, let's look into the depot — Open, Edit. What does it look like? What data is there? First there's a depot number. A depot type — a customer depot.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.000
There are various depot types on the bank's side. They label depot types differently. Then we see maybe fees are assigned.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.000
If someone has a certain depot, you can assign a certain fee model — that he pays so much for Swiss applications, for French applications, etc. So it's controlled at the depot.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.000
And controlled elsewhere too, but here as well. And then there's the new process I mentioned earlier — advance exemption. If he takes part in this new process, there's a mark (Fleck).

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.000
A very far-reaching mark, even if it's just a small mark in the depot. Very important. But also important is this power-of-attorney history (Vollmachtsverlauf) on the depot.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.000
A customer can grant the bank a power of attorney (Vollmacht) — that the bank may reclaim everything for him. If this exists, the customer barely has to worry about anything.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.000
If the power of attorney doesn't exist — and that happens. Some banks don't do it in certain countries. And some banks don't do it with power of attorney at all. Then the bank constantly sends the customer documents.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.000
And the customer has to review, sign himself, and send back. You can do that. But at the depot such a power of attorney is set up.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:28.000
And this power of attorney at the depot massively influences our processes. It's the entire print process we saw earlier — it reorients completely. I go to Open and edit.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.000
With this one mark. If I have no mark in there, he has no power of attorney. If he has none, we write to the customer. So our process is directed toward the customer.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.000
If I have a check in here, then the process bypasses the customer.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.000
He barely gets documents anymore — instead it goes straight to the customer's tax office and then to the foreign authority. So it goes different ways.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.000
That's what this one mark can trigger. There are other parameters that play in too. But — power of attorney is important at the depot. Right.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:33.000
And with that we're already at the depot. Of course a depot can also have income here. So you can look into the income data (Ertragsdaten).

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Now we're in the income data, on the depot. Before we were at 'General' on the depot. We're still in the depot.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.000
In the income data you see which income — better said, which securities — are held in this depot. But you also already see reclaimable amounts.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.000
So what's reclaimable per security? And this one actually has 943 rows. Some can.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.000
And then there's also a bank connection here. We saw that earlier. It's linked to the depot. That's the most important thing about the customer's depot itself for now.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Any questions on that? Good. Then I click OK to cancel.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.000
And we fall back to the customer. So we were at the customer, at Depot overview, and opened via double- or right-click. We work inside the depot.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Picture it always like this: new threads keep branching off. And then you can reach into the income data. Now I click Cancel again. OK.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Fell back to the customer, at his depot overview. What else is important?

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.000
That should do for the customer. Now let's go.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.000
There's a lot more contact data. Now I have the choice: dive deeper into the application settings, or show you the other contacts again.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.000
I'd say we go into the application settings. That's more important for the future. Stammdaten → Applikationseinstellungen. Okay.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Standard application settings. Here again there are various process trees that have little to do with each other, sometimes interlock, but are all very powerful. Let's stay on expiry deadlines (Ablauffristen).

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:46.000
I showed you the diagram. We remember this. This here basically mirrors that. Let's pull out Switzerland as before.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Here we see this term again, Switzerland at tax office Switzerland. And then there's a sentence: months after the end of the calendar year in which the interest/dividend was paid.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.000
Okay. When was the interest/dividend paid? Let's say 5/5/2020, last year. I'm making something up. Then you count the months after the end of the calendar year.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.000
So really, if I pick 5/5/2020, the calendar year has to expire. 2020 has to end. So from 1/1/2021 this deadline starts running.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.000
And then this deadline runs for 36 months, because it's set to 36 months. That's three years.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.000
So it runs three years. So you can still reclaim in 2021, 2022, 2023. In that window you may reclaim.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.000
2024 is over. Finito. Then the claim is time-barred. So here we have reclaims of three years.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Now this differs for various markets and modules. That one, for example, has 60 — Denmark.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:02.000
And Ireland, for example, has 48. But let's stay with the Swiss one: 36 months after the end of the calendar year in which the interest/dividend was paid,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.000
and a warning deadline of 180 and a custodian deadline. Wait — no, it has no custodian deadline. Nothing is entered.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.000
You don't have to enter anything, because in Switzerland there's no Lagerstelle — there we mostly file directly with the foreign tax authority. But there's still an official deadline duration.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.000
So looking at this, we wouldn't have this orange bar. We'd have the green bar, and this deadline, and the blue one. 36 would be the blue.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.000
180 is the warning deadline, the green one. And official deadline duration 14 would be the last 14 days after the limitation,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:35.000
so the case worker gets a notice that it's time-barred and is aware of it. Those are the deadlines. Then, earlier we touched the payment-receipt deadline.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.000
I won't go into that in detail either, but we know it exists. It's separately configurable here from the reclaims.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.000
Now let's go into the application-behavior settings (Anwendungseinstellungen). And since there are so many, we'll pull out two — so you get an idea how powerful it is.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:45.000
Here the first setting says: all application forms need authorized signatories. That's what it means — all application forms of all countries, whether Denmark, Ireland, Switzerland, France, no matter.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Authorized signatories have to be on it, from the case worker. Under power of attorney of course, if one exists. And if I set this to Yes, then for all forms it's decided:

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.000
authorized signatories get printed on. That's a very far-reaching default setting. If I set No, then of course not. What else do we have?

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.000
Use facsimile (Faksimile) in the application process. What's facsimile? Always digital signatures. We scan them in. From our case workers, if they want it

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.000
and support the process, even their signature. It's loaded into RaQuest and can then be printed onto the applications. We saw digital signatures earlier too.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.000
On the application, if you remember, there were signatures. And then we can say, yes, we use it by default, or no, we don't. So, turn it off/on for all applications. Very far-reaching.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.000
That's two already. But do we have another? Yes, I'll give one more: 'always fill in application questions' (Antragsfragen).

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.000
Did you see those little crosses on the Swiss one earlier? Those were application questions, always answered Yes/No. And we can decide whether they may, should, or shouldn't be filled in by default.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.000
And thereby with a Yes/No. If you set it to No, then the Swiss application — and all applications — simply get no crosses. This all has technical reasons

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.000
we discuss with our customers. Some want it one way, some the other. Now let's go a bit further into the application settings.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Gebühren (fees). Let's go first to fee profiles (Gebührenprofile). You can imagine, at the banks using RaQuest,

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.000
there are very different fees they charge their customers, and above all very complex fee models. I'm not even sure

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.000
we can go really deep today, but here for example there's a standard fee model, or profile. Double-click. Right-click, open, edit — that was the wrong one.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.000
Right-click, open, edit. Now you suddenly see, oh, there are several rows. Each row means a profile.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.000
And for the France market, 'CBL/CBF' means it's a bank. The bank is Clearstream, as I mentioned. So this here is a module.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.000
The fees concern a module: France market, for this Lagerstelle called Clearstream. And these fees are 60 euros in this case.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
60 euros. These fees are charged on the claim — the claims where we right-clicked → apply earlier, kicking off the process.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.000
On those claims 60 euros is charged immediately, if it concerns this module, if it's in the France market. That's still a relatively simple construct, and there are many more.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.000
If you double-click one of these rows, you see it's all gray now, because I may no longer change it. Many parameters to enter here. So I recommend: A, go through the manual once,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.000
B, study it in detail once you actually run into this topic professionally — because you'll have to, when you get a task on it. We can also deepen this in a separate training.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Just at the entry level. So you know: okay, different banks have different fee profiles. We split between standard fees

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.000
and country-specific fees. But when you go into the standard fees — that term reflects a profile,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.000
country-specific is another profile. And going into the standard fees you see, okay, in this 'standard fees' profile various fees are set, various markets, custodians,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.000
parameters, etc. You can even do it per institution. Here, for example, the institution we've been working with charges. Here's another partner institution in Germany, one in Luxembourg, with different fees

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:14.000
or maybe something more complex. Just to point you there — you find all this under Applikationseinstellungen. Now there's also a fee model (Gebührenmodell).

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.000
It's something at least one of our customers uses. They simply say, well, we have customers who can afford it and want a flat rate, an all-in fee.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.000
So they pay amount X per year and get everything all-inclusive. Easy. Then there are some where we say, on each claim we charge

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.000
a certain fee of 5 or 7 euros. Others are completely excluded from the withholding-tax service. So what we do with RaQuest — also called the withholding-tax service, reclaiming

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.000
withholding tax — is excluded, and they get nothing. No fees, but also no service. Nothing is done for them. They're frozen, so to speak.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.000
There's country-specific and standard too. So you can engage well with these fees, models and profiles here.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Let's advance to instrument classifications (Instrumentklassifizierung). What's that? It's so important because we constantly import securities, and these securities are also

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.000
clustered, so to speak. On these financial markets there are tons of really wild or fascinating security-type constructs. Say it's a

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.000
REIT — real-estate something. Or it has to do with pensions, or it's a participation certificate (Genussschein), a convertible bond. You can read up on it wonderfully on Wikipedia,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.000
what's all out there. Government bond — these are all names of instrument types out there. This list can get very, very long at many customers. But they mostly all have in common,

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:42.000
they trace back to being either shares or bonds. So here we have a link to a product group, which we call share or bond. And then we go one tree level up into the product group, where

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.000
shares/bonds are matched to security types, and in the end they're all securities. Up top it's all securities. This tree or these grids can be extended at will, depending

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.000
on requirement. But that's the pattern generally recognized and lived. So when a

00:08:49.000 --> 00:09:00.000
government bond comes in, the term 'government bond' first appears, but we identify it as a bond. If we identify it as a bond, it's not calculated the same as a share. Shares are mostly calculated in units,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.000
and for bonds I don't know the exact technical term. It's simply a matching table — picture it — which also becomes very important.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:18.000
We were already at the base typifications earlier, and at the legal forms. Just to jog the memory.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.000
Right. Now let's build up a little theory. We could say customer typifications are like sand on the beach out there — all these many different technical terms across many different markets and countries.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.000
It's simply a table where they're collected. Only some of them, say three of them, are only for the France market. That's not really how it is, but I just want to

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:24.000
illustrate it vividly. Some are for the Switzerland market, some for the Germany market. We have no matching, no assignment yet. How do we do it? We go top-left to

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.000
investment markets, pick any market, say France, and there we have 'permissible customer typifications' — there they are again,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
but far fewer now. So this France market has been allotted 35 customer typifications. So these typifications were matched to the France market, and these terms are used for France, or better said

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.000
for the forms at the end of the day. If you picture such a printed form and it suddenly says 'Foundation', then

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:33.000
this term comes from the database, from this link. Here the deadlines are reflected again — you know those already —

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.000
and then we're simply back in the withholding-tax rates we've already seen and learned. I click Cancel. Cancel again.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Let me look briefly. Four-eyes principle we've done, Webparts we've done, menu we've done, we've gone through an application process twice

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.000
and seen others. Payment receipts are now a concept. Application settings, securities are concepts. Quarantine, critical reclaims, warning documents are concepts. That's all very good.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.000
Excellent. Then let's make a short detour. Let it sink in — with the application

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.000
settings, there's much more. We won't manage it all today. Nor do we need to. Now, some background on what we have in terms of modules. If you

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.000
clicked this line — I'll do it — a rather inconspicuous window opens; I'll close it again. Or if you click top-right

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.000
on the '?' Info, you get info about the system first. You get the version number — reminding us again of the March 18th version.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.000
The Flavor is KPMG, so we're working on a KPMG flavor, which also does RaQuest withholding-tax service for one, two or three banks

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.000
using RaQuest. Then we see the operating system is Microsoft Windows, and there are local reports — what does that mean?

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
All forms, all applications are managed locally on my machine, or locally where the instance is installed.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.000
Then we go to Database — it's called RQ Demo, on SQL '...Ferry'. That's probably

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.000
the server, I'm not sure, but it's the database name. Very good — I often do this when I have a support case: I ask, please look into

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Info, tell me the current version number — standard procedure if we don't already know. Up top are tabs, there are the license

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
keys. Down here there are several pages you can flip through, and you already see how many license keys are in here. It's not yet

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.000
all that exist, but they split into tax domiciles (Steuerdomizile) and markets (Märkte). So RaQuest doesn't work if

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.000
there's no license key in it. And if you have tax domiciles in — Belgium, Luxembourg, France, Switzerland — then the domiciles are at least covered. Domicile means:

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.000
I, Christoph, living in Germany, registered at Rosenheim tax office, need the domicile Germany. What do markets mean? Markets, put very generally, is a

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.000
separate license key to unlock a market in general. I think the deadlines we heard earlier hang off that. And so on. But much more interesting

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
are the application modules (Antragsmodule). Here again you see these abbreviations, combinations of abbreviations, and reading them: SE stands for Sweden. Sweden at tax office

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.000
Sweden, Norway at tax office Norway, Italy at CBL/CBF — I know by heart, Clearstream. Clearstream is a bank and a Lagerstelle. I'll keep repeating it so it sticks.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.000
Or Denmark at tax office Denmark. So only with these modules and markets can customers file. We earn money on every module, and we maintain every module. Suddenly a

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.000
market decides there'll be a new application form with three new fields — then we have to build them in. The market is maintained, it's delivered, and then he can start with the new documents.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Those are the application modules. And going further — nothing there — there are also advance-exemption modules (Vorbefreiungsmodule), probably in here. Under

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.000
Modules, advance-exemption modules — those are the modules for the other process, the advance-exemption process. So you can, may

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.000
and must keep checking which licenses are actually in the application. Only with these licenses loaded do the processes work.

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.000
The other two things don't interest us. Then we can close this mask again. So: always click either the name 'RaQuest Kumamoto' or the line to the right of it,

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.000
the black line, and you get into this system information, can switch between the tabs, or you go to '?' Info. That gets you in too.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.000
What we haven't seen — a shame — the background activities are down. Here lots of calculation would happen. Whenever you do something in RaQuest, a row appears, another row, another

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.000
row — hundreds, thousands of rows. It means nothing other than the system signaling: something's being calculated here, rules are being applied, and with these rules

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.000
all claims or customer data — whatever — are moved into the new state, so everything is rule-compliant. Sometimes complex calculations have to run.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.000
I call it traceability. Then rows crop up there — I can also show it in a separate application —

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.000
and then you can see whether there's an error. And if there's an error, this happens. I'll show you a diagram. Little circles appear — a green check means all good; if there's an error, it says something is blocked

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.000
or finished with an error. And if it finished with an error, you should look closer and investigate. Then maybe you can help the customer and resolve it. There are other

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:53.000
circles too. Here, when this background process is running and running and doing its thing, then you get the icon; and there's also the letter,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.000
meaning an action is being pre-calculated and the letter is waiting — some action is waiting its turn. So nothing alarming. Those are the background activities

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.000
you'll get to know soon. Now I'd say we have

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.000
the chance to look briefly into the institution — the actual financial institution that operates RaQuest — because there are many very interesting settings there.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.000
I'd put a lid on it today for the training, and there's still lots to train, and we have to go deeper into every topic to understand it better. But let's open the institution again. How? I go to Kontakte,

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
Kontakte verwalten, and top-right there's Kontaktart, and I select Finanzinstitut, Suche starten. It shows me various

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.000
institutions — because a bank could have several institutions — or they could be asset-management companies that in turn manage funds.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.000
But I need the very first one; the very first is usually the Mandant (tenant), the most powerful institution with the most powerful configurations and settings. Double-click. Here again you see the address, no different from a contact.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:07.000
But here there's a word: Bevollmächtigungen (authorizations). Only an institution can decide for which markets it even offers the

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:10.000
withholding-tax service. And if you say, for the Belgium market, Canada, Switzerland, blah blah, I want that, then I have to

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.000
switch on the green checks. And it applies either to all customers, or to person/group/organisation/fund. You could also exclude funds for Switzerland. If it's not on, it's not on. Or if

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.000
the institution doesn't want to support it, it's not supported. Takes a moment while it calculates. So these institution-level authorization settings say, fundamentally,

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.000
which markets we do at all. If, for example, Japan isn't checked here, then — 

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
even if the module is loaded (as we just saw with modules), even if it's loaded and the authorization here isn't active — I can't file under power of attorney. The claims are there, but you

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.000
can't do much with them yet. You won't even find the function to do anything. Of course an institution has

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:23.000
tax data. What else? It also has bank connections, obviously. But here it's usually not about

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.000
the institution having a bank connection — rather these are the accounts we set up for when the foreign institutions or authorities — the custodians and authorities — transfer the money

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.000
from abroad; it comes in here on the various accounts. So in RaQuest we have to store the accounts at the institution. And these accounts too

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.000
have account roles: Clearing accounts, money-receipt accounts (Geldeingangskonto). Clearing means nothing other than: this is where the money comes, where it's transferred to.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.000
Money-receipt account is a second role you can go deeper into when the time comes. I'll leave it

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.000
at that. But a small question — I mean, a case worker could theoretically destroy quite a lot in the system too, right? If we just remove a few

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.000
checkmarks and then... — No, he won't be able to, because a case worker has roles. — Right. But some have more roles, don't they? Then he's also authorized to do it. And if he makes mischief,

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.000
he gets in trouble not from us but from his bosses. As a rule there's also a lively exchange via tickets, Jira tickets. Say someone wants to

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
test something. If he tests in the test environment — they have test environments — it's not dramatic. He can do as he likes there. Then maybe he gets a note from Bogdan on our side.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:34.000
With such-and-such role you can unlock such-and-such rights, but be careful, because this happens. Please don't do it in production — that'd be a hint. And whoever has the

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.000
rights was assigned them because he should and must carry them out. But a normal case worker may not make these settings, can't make them.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:35.000
He really only does the filing of applications, checks everything fits, looks into the quarantine, edits things, phones around, prints. And when the big blunder

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.000
comes — or do we move on to the topic of administrators, so they can set something. It happens, but even if he — Claudia, did you want to say something? — I just wanted to say

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.000
that the administration areas aren't even displayed. So he doesn't even have the option to misclick there. — Ah, I see. That's right, Christoph? A case worker

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.000
doesn't have the option to misclick, shouldn't. Even if he's very powerful with very many settings — incredibly many — a case worker shouldn't.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.000
But that was still within her authority, within her rights concept, all fine. It was simply a user error (Anwenderfehler). But there's a big

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.000
permissions concept — I'll show briefly. I go to RaQuest's main page, Administration, Berechtigungen verwalten (manage permissions), and Jörg gets through this well too, I hope.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.000
You can click through the processes and say: who may make bookings, who may reverse bookings — double-click, and you see: the expert

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.000
may do this, the case worker, auditors may do that. One may execute, another has full access, the next has full access, and the next may only write and execute. It's really designed down to

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.000
the functions. So the view doesn't change depending on your role. It's always the same — you just can't perform certain actions.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:44.000
Or you get a notification when you try, or nothing. Often you just don't see the function. In other cases he can't perform it. But a message, I think, he rarely gets —

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.000
actually not at all. What one might add: case workers in the withholding-tax field really know their stuff. So what

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.000
they're allowed to do, they also know — I'd say in 80% of cases — what to do with it. I think.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:47.000
And to wrap this up a bit — we can do one more Q&A. Back to the institution: here too, for the advance-exemption process, there's

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:47.000
the option to say, fundamentally we want to do advance exemption for persons, groups, organisations, funds, these markets and these custodians.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:48.000
So it's a general, global setting, and only then does it all work. Right. There's a lot more in RaQuest, things we still have to get to know,

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.000
but for the kickoff, for the start, that should do. When someone really gets into the application, I can only recommend: click, click, click, think, understand, learn, ask, ask, ask — just don't hesitate

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:50.000
to call people. Claudia, me, Markus, developers too, support, quality management — who's very good with the app: Sven, and

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.000
by now Chana, Jörg, are really gifted at these things. I'd just recommend grabbing people when you don't understand something, especially

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.000
since we work a lot remotely from home office right now — don't hesitate, just call and have it explained. — How do I get to RaQuest? Just type it online, RaQuest.de?

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.000
— Or give me a link to log in. Good question. Let me log out briefly. I always go there and have a link, and please only in the

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.000
Internet Explorer, this blue Internet Explorer down here. Only in it does it work at the moment. Maybe soon we'll do other browsers. Right now only that one works. And if you got a link, say I give you

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.000
this link, you'd open it and it works. Secondly, you'd have to install a local instance if you want. It's a very elaborate process, which Claudia and Markus

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:55.000
can now do and show you, and after that you'd be able to open an application again. But I can do it via the link.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.000
If you say installation — I generally recommend doing the installation, especially at Bogdan's, because you need it again and again, since you keep having to spin up a new instance.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.000
For various customers. — Claudia, briefly, is it the demo we use for Sales? — I don't know. It's like this. The background to all this

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.000
is that every bank runs its own instance. So RaQuest isn't hosted in some cloud — every bank has its own instance installed on its own server.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.000
And as Christoph said, spinning up a local instance is quite sensible. There are also server instances — that's for example what Christoph is on right now. So server instances at RaQuest

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.000
internally. I think that's the one we use for Sales. I hope I didn't break anything for you, Claudia. — Not for me. I'll just reset it, or have Jörg reset it.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.000
I have so many links now, right? And these are my local instances. Right. — Does the sandbox (Spielwiese) still work?

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.000
— Don't know, I don't use it. Let's see, because then you could click around in it; otherwise we'd have to give you a server version maybe.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.000
— No, the sandbox doesn't exist anymore. Oh right, it's on Erkundvold 3, that doesn't work. Now I just have to check whether this one test instance

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.000
I have is local. Otherwise you can use it. Otherwise I'd say, again check with Jörg, who spins up these instances — all these sandbox instances —

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.000
where you just say, you divide them among yourselves. Because you'd really need to be able to play around in those things and break nothing. You can't break anything there.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.000
Right. Then, on my part, thank you for being so attentive. You have the video — Claudia can surely give you the video — where you can always jump back to the spots. Whenever you want to orient yourself again,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.000
the video is available afterwards.

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:08.000
